=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r1
diagnosis_text:
Router r1 has a complete FRR configuration file (`/etc/frr/frr.conf`) that declares OSPF participation on its internal links (10.0.0.32/30 to csw1, 10.0.0.36/30 to csw2) and includes `default-information originate` to redistribute its kernel default route into the OSPF domain. However, the FRR package is not installed on r1: no binary exists at `/usr/sbin/frr*` or `/usr/lib/frr/`, `ospfd`/`zebra`/`vtysh` are absent from PATH, there is no `daemons` configuration file, and the runit service directory (`/etc/sv/`) contains only `dnsmasq` and `ssh` — no FRR service entry. As a result, r1 never starts an OSPF process, never forms adjacencies with csw1 or csw2 (confirmed: csw1 has five full OSPF neighbours, none of which is r1's router-ID 10.0.0.76), and never injects the default route. Every interior router — csw1, csw2, dswa1, dswa2, dswb1, dswb2 — therefore has no route to any internet destination. Internet-bound packets from clients (e.g., pc1 at 10.1.0.100) are dropped at the first-hop distribution switch (dswa1) with ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable". The user's reported slow/hanging behaviour is consistent with TCP-layer retry and timeout cycles against a silently black-holed internet path. The root cause is that the FRR package was never installed on r1, leaving a valid OSPF configuration file with no daemon to execute it. Fix: install the `frr` package on r1, add a runit service entry for it under `/etc/sv/frr`, and start the service; once zebra and ospfd are running, r1 will form OSPF adjacencies with csw1/csw2, and `default-information originate` will propagate the existing kernel default route (via ispa at 203.0.113.1) throughout the routing domain.
=== END ===